      "narrative": "The Copper Finch's door groans open and the warmth hits you first \u2014 a peat fire crackling in the hearth, the smell of old ale and wood smoke. The tavern is sparser than when you left. A few patrons huddle at the far end near the fire, nursing mugs in low conversation.\n\nYour old table stands empty, tucked against the wall where the lamplight pools amber on scarred wood. A half-burned candle sits in a clay dish, its wax long cold.\n\nThe moment you settle into the chair \u2014 the wood familiar beneath you \u2014 the door to the back room swings open. Mara Venn steps out, a fresh mug in her hand. She stops when she sees you, tilts her head, then walks over and sets the mug down in front of you without being asked.\n\n\"You look like you found something,\" she says, pulling out the chair across from you. She doesn't sit, just rests a hand on its back and waits, her sharp eyes reading your face."
